  "answer": "Mendeleev’s periodic table had several limitations. One major limitation was the presence of anomalous pairs of elements, where elements with higher atomic weights were placed before those with lower atomic weights. For example, tellurium (Te) with an atomic weight of 127.6 was placed before iodine (I) with an atomic weight of 126.9. Mendeleev accepted these minor inversions because they resulted in elements being placed in the correct groups. Another limitation was the placement of dissimilar elements in the same group, such as alkali metals (like Li, Na, K) and coinage metals (like Cu, Ag, Au), which have very different properties. Additionally, Mendeleev’s table did not account for the atomic number of elements, which led to inconsistencies in the arrangement of elements. The modern periodic table, introduced by H.J. Moseley, addresses these limitations by arranging elements based on their atomic numbers rather than atomic weights. This change eliminated the problem of anomalous series and allowed for a more accurate prediction of element properties. The modern periodic table also provides a better understanding of the electronic configurations of elements, which explains their chemical and physical properties more effectively.",
